    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The core assertion regarding TemporalTrans's efficacy in capturing long-range temporal dependencies warrants a more granular examination of the experimental setup. While the reported MSE reductions on ETT and Weather are encouraging, the reported ablation studies appear somewhat **insufficient** to definitively isolate the contribution of the adaptive attention mechanism itself. Specifically, the authors should delineate a comparison against attention variants that address sequence length scalability differently to rigorously test the novelty claimed in the title.\n\nA more substantial concern revolves around the robustness of the model across diverse time-series characteristics. The selection of ETT and Weather, while standard, may not fully represent the heterogeneous nature of real-world complex systems. The current validation seems to suffer from a potential **oversight** regarding model generalization; we would benefit from seeing performance metrics on datasets exhibiting different levels of stationarity or periodicity.\n\nFurthermore, the theoretical framing linking linear complexity to dependency capture feels somewhat hand-wavy. The explanation of *how* the linear scaling facilitates superior dependency aggregation is currently **unclear** and leans heavily on speculation rather than mathematical derivation or detailed mechanistic insight. Providing a deeper look into the attention weight distributions across varying look-back windows would explicitly address this gap.\n\nIn summary, the current presentation suggests a promising",
      "source": "target",
