    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The current experimental design exhibits a potential methodological flaw when extrapolating performance across different domains, which raises serious questions about broader applicability. Specifically, the connection between linear complexity improvements and robust long-range dependency modeling appears **contradictory**; simply achieving lower computational overhead does not inherently validate the claimed theoretical superiority in modeling distant temporal correlations.\n\nTo resolve this, the authors must clarify why the scaling mechanism bypasses known information bottleneck issues common in sequence-to-sequence modeling. Without this detailed exposition, the empirical successes risk being an artifact of the specific datasets chosen, indicating a potential **limitations** in the model's fundamental design for highly irregular time series.\n\nOverall, while the novelty of TemporalTrans is acknowledged, the manuscript currently presents more suggestive evidence than conclusive proof. The confluence of insufficiently deep ablation studies and the ambiguity surrounding the mechanism of long-range capture suggests the current form of the paper is more speculative than rigorously substantiated.",
